   253	P0(D)(exp(theta_0)F).G
   254	 =exp(theta_0) P0(D_x+delta,D_t-p0(delta))F.G
   255	 =exp(theta_0) P(D_x,D_t)F.G.
   256	```
   257	
   258	The auxiliary phase has no y dependence, so the same relation holds with D_y. Therefore F,G solve the mixed system.
   259	
   260	The constant term of F comes from the auxiliary singleton and is 1; the constant term of G is the empty subset. An F-type physical singleton comes from Fplus without the auxiliary index and has amplitude A_i. A G-type singleton comes from the balanced auxiliary-F/physical-G pair in G0. Its coefficient is
   261	
   262	`((r_i-delta)/(r_i+delta)) * A_i*lambda*k_i/(lambda*k_i-1) = A_i`.
   263	
   264	Its F coefficient is zero by charge counting. Thus all generic one-sided seed patterns and independent active amplitudes are realized, not merely a constrained subclass.
   265	
   266	## 6. Why the coefficient formula includes c3=0
   267	
   268	For a term selected by the charge projection, its full index set T (possibly including the auxiliary index) has `m` F-type and `n` G-type entries with `m=n` or `m=n+1`. In the spectral product R_T(r),
   269	
   270	- each same-type pair contributes `lambda*Q_ij`;
   271	- each opposite-type pair contributes `lambda^(-1)*Q_ij`;
   272	- each G amplitude contributes `lambda*A_i*k_i/(lambda*k_i-1)`.
   273	
   274	The total power of lambda is
   275	
   276	`binomial(m,2)+binomial(n,2)-m*n+n = (m-n)(m-n-1)/2 = 0`.
   277	
   278	After canceling these powers **symbolically**, the remaining terms are precisely §2. The choice of T is determined by charge: F uses S when `d_S=1` and `0+S` when `d_S=0`; G uses S when `d_S=0` and `0+S` when `d_S=-1`.
   279	
   280	The coefficients in §2 are rational functions of lambda regular at zero on the stated generic k,l domain. Moreover `c3=(2*c2/3)*lambda`, and both physical dispersions are polynomial in lambda. Every bilinear coefficient is therefore a rational function regular at zero. It vanishes for generic nonzero lambda by §5, hence is the zero rational function and vanishes at lambda=0 as well. This proves the **quadratic branch for arbitrary N**, not just a fixed-N limit calculation.
   281	
   282	## 7. Genericity, uniqueness, and diagonal reduction
   283	
   284	For generic nonzero k and l, `p(k)+p(-k)=2*c2*k^2 != 0`, so the one-soliton equations force exactly the two one-sided seed types used here. We did not discard a generic two-amplitude branch: such a branch belongs to odd p, not `c2 != 0`.
   285	
   286	For each subset with at least two elements, neither `P(kappa_S)` nor `P(-kappa_S)` is the zero polynomial in the free k's. For `deg p=d`, their top-degree part contains a mixed monomial `k_i^(d-1)*k_j` with coefficient `+d*c_d` or `-d*c_d`; the individual dispersion contributions contain no such mixed monomial. Thus the determinant nonvanishing conditions, and all displayed coefficient denominators, hold on a nonempty generic domain. The same is true after `l_i=k_i`. On that domain the shared recursion is unique, so the explicit solution proves its generic all-N test passes for every type pattern.
   287	
   288	For the reduction, choose `l_i=k_i`. The final F,G depend on x,y through `x+y`, so D_y and D_x coincide on the pair. Restricting to y=0 yields the (1+1)-dimensional system with the same coefficients. This is not the erroneous substitution `F(x,x,t)` without rescaling.
